[{:seon.task/name "definitions-from-the-compiler"
  :seon.task/plan "Each top-level form's definitions come from Jolt's analyzer (`jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*`): vars by kind, methods [multi dispatch], protocol impls [protocol type], the ns form and its requires, resolved refs (vars, macros, classes), arities, exact text; readback after the eval decides what actually ran. Applied per form through the run-ahead writer (225 us, flat). Replaces `seon.program/def-heads`, `def-form?`, recording's form tests, `names-in`, `ns-env`, stage's flagged guesses and the request-level :defs buffer in seon.repl. Design: notes/archive/audit-2026-10-21/program-facts-draft.edn (133/133 definitions agree with the runtime on 2,016 real agent forms). Main loads forms in the topological order of the real refs and ns requires."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-form-records-exactly-what-the-compiler-defined
                      seon.program-test/defmethod-extend-and-defrecord-merge-and-load-on-main
                      seon.program-cost-test/recording-a-form-is-flat-in-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "program-mutations-are-refused-at-merge"
  :seon.task/plan "Program code (what merges) does not call clojure.core/intern, alter-var-root, eval or load-string (loading code is an effect), and defines nothing inside a fn body (inline-def). The done rule refuses the branch naming the definition and the call, from the compiler's resolved refs (never text). The REPL still evaluates them; the merge judges. Platform and library code are not judged. (AGENTS §5: intern and alter-var-root are code smells; docs/resolution.md.)"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-program-calling-alter-var-root-is-refused-naming-it
                      seon.program-test/a-def-inside-a-fn-body-is-refused}}
 {:seon.task/name "tests-run-by-reach"
  :seon.task/plan "Results are reused by input digest (AGENTS §5). What exists: `seon.program/reach-digests` gives each test its reach digest: its own form, its namespace's ns form and non-defining forms, and every live form its `:form/refs` reach transitively (a var to the form that last defined it, a multimethod's methods, a protocol's extensions), each digested once (`seon.error/digest`), plus the checkout's `:env` digest (every tracked file but src/test .clj, task files and prose: deps.edn, bin/, resources/, test fixtures, every submodule pin, Jolt's included), plus the whole tree's digest for a test reaching a tree reader (`seon.program/tree-readers`). `bin/test` (test/test_runner.clj) imports src and test as program facts (`seon.source/import-tx`), reuses every green result stored at its digest and runs the rest. Results live in one content-addressed directory shared by every checkout (`:seon.config.source/test-results-dir`: one file per digest, written temp-then-rename through `seon.fx/write-file`), not the store, since a checkout's store cannot be shared across worktrees. A test's digest moves only when a form it reaches does (`seon.program/reaching-tests` is the inverse closure). v1 (`~/src/seon` seon.test.cache) keyed a result by reach digest; its lesson: inputs without program facts are folded into every digest, never left out. Known gaps: a test reading a file by a reader outside `tree-readers` or resolving a var by name at run time is digested without it; importing the program costs about 10 s per run (incremental import by file is next)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-changed-function-changes-the-digests-of-exactly-the-tests-reaching-it
                      seon.program-test/a-second-run-with-nothing-changed-runs-nothing
                      seon.program-test/a-result-recorded-in-one-checkout-is-reused-in-another}}
 {:seon.task/name "refs-name-quoted-symbols-and-macro-uses"
  :seon.task/plan "Done. `seon.program/unit-account` records a form's :refs from the analyzer's IR, and `my.agent/callers`, `seon.program/reaching-tests` and the reach digests (docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach) trust them. They missed a syntax-quoted or quoted qualified symbol (`seon.render.ui/system-page`'s tile renders, `'seon.check/probe-proc`, every :seon.render/html or :malli/schema symbol resolved by name) and a macro use (`seon.sock/with-mem`, `seon.config/baked-schema-forms`), so `my.agent/callers` reported about 35 live functions as uncalled and a test reaching a function only through a quoted name kept a stale green digest. Now: `seon.program/quoted-vars` in `unit-account` adds a :quote or :const datum's qualified symbols whose var exists; `seon.program/macro-uses` in `seon.program/account` reads each list whose head resolves to a macro var from the form's own text (the tap's unit of a top-level macro call expanding to a do names no macro, and Jolt's analyzer keeps no macro on the node, so no Jolt change); Clojure's own macros are recorded too. `seon.program/reach-digests` adds each :schema/key entry as a node, with edges from a contracted var's form to the entries its contract names and between entries (`seon.program/names`). Measured on src and test (330 tests, 2,236 live forms): editing `seon.lib/defc`'s body moved 298 digests, 0 before; :form/refs 28,344 to 33,916 datoms; import about 8 s either way; digests 602 to 705 ms. Open gaps (docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach): a quoted name whose var does not exist at analysis; `seon.program/reaching-tests` still follows refs only (40 tests for the defc edit against 298 digests); bin/test imports no schema resources and keeps them in :env. Once callers is trusted, the brief rule 'confirm with grep across src, test, resources, bin and docs' (notes/archive/worktrees/brief-common.md step 4) can go."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-quoted-or-syntax-quoted-var-is-a-ref
                      seon.program-test/a-macro-use-is-a-ref
                      seon.program-test/a-test-reaching-a-quoted-function-changes-digest-with-it
                      seon.program-test/a-changed-macro-moves-the-digest-of-each-test-that-expands-it
                      seon.program-test/a-changed-contract-schema-moves-the-digest-of-tests-reaching-the-contracted-var}}
 {:seon.task/name "compiled-units-in-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "Deferred, kept open (DECISIONS: Compiled units in the store: deferred): not to be released until reopened; its proof is what reopening must make green. Nothing compiles at an agent's fork (copy-on-write from the fork server, fork-ms p50 0.68 ms) or in a root boot beyond `seon.program/load-all!`'s catch-up of forms the binary lacks (notes/archive/store-native-2026-10-22.md#q1). A real form loads in 1.6-5 ms, ~3.6 ms of it Jolt's analysis and emit plus Chez's compile (what a stored unit would replace, by a 0.01 ms `load-compiled-from-port`) and 1-2 ms contract arming (which stays). A Chez unit is deterministic bytes, 1.7-2.6 KB for a 450 B form; Jolt's AOT units are 15x their source with absolute paths, and every Jolt or Chez pin bump strands them. Reopen when point-in-time loading is measured as the bottleneck: per-form units keyed by (form text, what it reaches, runtime fingerprint: seon.db/each-commit-names-its-runtime), after Jolt emits one form to a bytevector with no absolute paths. The larger cost on that path is macro fan-out: `load!` of 67 seon.lib forms with their dependents took 2,367 ms against 109 ms for the forms alone (`seon.program/dependents`)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/each-commit-names-its-runtime"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-cost-test/loading-a-form-from-its-stored-unit-costs-its-arming}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-repl-at-a-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "A branch is rebuilt from its definitions; evaluations are history. Already true (notes/archive/store-native-2026-10-22.md#q3): `seon.program/load-all!`, `load-changes!` and `load-diff!` read live forms' :form/src, and nothing re-runs an evaluation. Missing: a REPL on the program as of commit C, made by forking the fork server's image, `d/commit-as-db` at C (0.7-0.9 ms) and `load-diff!` to it (1.6-5 ms per differing form). It restores code and data defs exactly (main: 1,119 code forms, 87 literal and 56 computed defs); host cells (7 atoms/delays) come back empty and host objects (ffi libraries, LMDB, sockets, procs, threads) are made fresh, as at boot; an agent's REPL state outside definitions stays with `clone!`. Waits for the owner's go."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.agent/an-agents-base-is-its-merge-base"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-repl-at-a-commit-holds-that-commits-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: a root started on target/seon built from src, then src/seon/program.clj edited (one defn removed, one docstring changed), with :seon.config.source/dirs [\"src\" \"test\"] (`seon.agent-test/in-agent`): in the agent (a fork of the fork server after the import), `(seon.program/reach-digests ...)` answers \"Attempting to call unbound fn: #'seon.program/reach-digests\", an unchanged var of the changed file, and `seon.agent/test-plan` selects nothing (four seon.agent-test tests red, one root hung in stop!). Rebuilt from the same tree, all are green. So loading a changed file's forms into a process whose binary holds the file (`seon.program/load-changes!`, `seon.fork-server/server-step` :tx, which runs every file's ns form first) leaves a var it did not change unbound. Cause to find, at its owner: the ns form's evaluation, `seon.program/remove-definition!` of the removed var, or the load of the changed form. Correct: a changed file loads its changed forms and leaves every other var as the binary holds it. Related: seon.source/an-import-reads-a-changed-ns-form-before-its-forms. Until fixed, bin/test builds target/seon before anything runs (docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-vars-history-is-read-at-the-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the store keeps history (`:seon.config.store/keep-history?`, docs/storage.md#history) and a var's definitions are facts on its branch (:var/form, :form/source; `seon.program/live-forms` reads the live ones), but no function reads a var's past definitions. v1 had `my.program/history`. Correct: one function an agent calls with a symbol, beside `my.agent/callers` and `my.agent/callees`, answering each definition the var has had on its branch: the form's text, its transaction and time, from Datahike's history db over the var's :var/form datoms; printed by the one printer, every cut naming how to read the rest. Adds no record; replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-vars-history-names-each-definition-it-had}}
 {:seon.task/name "bin-test-imports-its-schemas"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `bin/test` (test/test_runner.clj) imports src and test as program facts (`seon.source/import-tx`) but no schema resources, so schemas reach its tests' reach digests only through :env, which digests all of resources/ (docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach): a change to one schema moves every test's digest. Root's import writes them (`seon.source/schema-tx`). Correct: bin/test imports the schema resources as root does, so a schema key is a program key whose dependents' digests move (`seon.program/schema-dependents`), and resources/seon/schemas leaves :env. Measure the digests a one-schema change moves, before and after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-changed-schema-moves-only-the-digests-reaching-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-method-key-is-decoded-once"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a method's dispatch value is encoded twice. `seon.program` stores {:dispatch (sedn/encode dispatch)}, and `seon.program/method-key` encodes [multi that-string] again. `remove-definition!` then reads the inner string back with clojure.core/read-string (program.clj:646), not `seon.edn/decode`. This is audit row C10's remnant: DUP-ROOT moved the outer decodes (program.clj:447, :705) onto seon.edn. Correct: the dispatch value is encoded once inside `method-key`, and every read goes through `seon.edn/decode`. Deletes: the inner encode and the read-string."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-removed-method-with-a-vector-dispatch-is-gone}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-forms-facts-are-written-by-one-function"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.program/account-tx` (program.clj:384-408) and seon.merge's set-form-tx (merge.clj:182-200) each write a form's facts with the same eight (seq x) (assoc :form/x ..) lines (defines, declares, refs, requires, banned, inline-defs, timeouts, catches) and each carries the same Datahike tempid workaround (program.clj:388, merge.clj:168). A new form attribute is two edits that can disagree. Correct: one `seon.program` function from a form's account to its row; account-tx and set-form-tx call it, and `seon.program/form-row` reads the same attribute list. Deletes one chain and one workaround (~45 lines)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-merged-form-holds-what-an-imported-form-holds}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-closure-over-a-graph"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: hand-written transitive walks in `seon.program/dependents` (:541), `seon.program/schema-dependents` (:565, a fixpoint that rescans every schema key each round: O(K^2)) and `seon.program/reach-digests` (:809), and `seon.task/accumulate`'s order (task.clj:213-216, (some #{id} acc) per visit: O(n^2)). Correct: one `closure [start neighbors]` beside `seon.program/topo`, a set for seen; each caller passes its neighbors. Not this: Datahike's experimental transitive-closure (not in our build, a six-method GraphSpec, O(E) lookups). Write the cost proof first."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-cost-test/schema-dependents-grows-with-what-it-reaches}}
 {:seon.task/name "load-dependencies-leave-quoted-names-out"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by jolt/writeback-order's review (2026-10-23): ```seon.program/unit-account``` adds the vars a form's quoted data names (quoted-vars) to :refs, beside the vars it evaluates, and ```seon.program/load-order``` reads every :refs entry as a load dependency. So seon.db/install-missing!, which names 'seon.db/open! in an error's data, was ordered after open!, which calls it (12 of src's files). load-order now keeps the order between two forms read from a file, but a form recorded at a REPL that quotes a later name still gets a phantom edge, and a cycle through one is broken at the wrong form. Correct, at the producer: the account keeps the quoted names apart (the reach and the context's callers read both; load-order reads only the evaluated ones), from the analyzer's :quote/:const nodes as now. Proof: load-order of a REPL-recorded form quoting a later var keeps its order."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/load-order-reads-no-quoted-name}}]
 {:seon.task/name "an-import-reads-each-form-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23, task seon.derive/one-content-keyed-derivation's cold paths). seon.source/import-tx of src and test (115 files, 2,409 forms) measured 541M ticks, 3,553 MB and 640k contracted calls cold, about 1.5 MB a form. Where it goes, by phase over the whole tree (warm): Jolt's analyzer 243M ticks and 1,150 MB (~2.6 KB an IR node, 442k nodes); unit-account 75M and 717 MB, of which seon.program/quoted-vars 28M and 492 MB over 140k constants (3.5 KB each: tree-seq, a set and its contract on every keyword and string); account 95M and 697 MB, with macro-uses reading the form's text a second time (27M, 119 MB) and tree-seq walking it (Jolt's tree-seq is ~1.1 KB a node visited); read-forms 34M and 141 MB; account-tx 28M and 148 MB; hasch form ids 17M and 142 MB. Fixed here, at their cause: a scalar constant that is no qualified symbol is not walked (unit-account), and account takes the form as read (:form, required by its contract; seon.source/accounts and seon.repl pass theirs), so macro-uses walks it and the text is read once. After: 497M ticks, 3,213 MB, 519k calls cold (-8%, -10%, -19%). Not fixed here, each its own task: Jolt's analyzer allocation per node (being measured); tree-seq's per-node cost (Jolt's clojure.core); 354 B per armed contracted call (seon.cost/units :fn/armed-call) on hot private helpers (vsym, datum, invoked: 5 calls per IR call node). Replaces the second read; deletes nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-cost-test/a-scalar-constant-costs-no-walk
                      seon.program-cost-test/an-account-reads-its-form-once}}]
